 "news": {
  "date": "2026-10-26",
  "asof": "2026-10-27 14:59 UTC",
  "headline": "Meta is raising $95 billion \u2014 the cash-flow question got its answer, and the answer is shareholders",
  "standfirst": "Meta launched a plan to raise up to $95bn of equity to fund AI infrastructure: $22.5bn of Class A in a forward sale, $12.5bn of mandatory convertible preferred, and an at-the-market programme of up to $60bn in its new non-voting Class C shares from the first quarter of 2027, with Nvidia, AMD and Broadcom interested in up to $5bn. Set against $825bn of future lease and contract commitments, Q3 free cash flow of minus $2.12bn \u2014 its first negative quarter in more than ten years \u2014 and $82.45bn of cash against $80.92bn of long-term debt. Four sessions after marking the stock down 6.98% for exactly that problem, the market took the answer and bought the complex: the S&P rose 0.70%, the Nasdaq 0.82% and the semiconductor index more than 3% back above 11,000, though it is still down about 12% in October. SpaceX secured $40bn of debt for GPU purchases and Anthropic filed publicly for a Nasdaq IPO under ANT, showing $16.3bn of first-half revenue and an $80.7bn net loss largely from non-cash convertible revaluations; Musk warned of guppy-level death and consolidation among smaller data-centre operators, citing tight debt markets. A $69bn two-year auction went badly while the ten-year eased to 4.99%. Oil fell a second day on diplomacy that agreed nothing \u2014 WTI $101.76, Brent $115.81 \u2014 as officials from Saudi Arabia, Turkey and Pakistan went to Tehran. In the book GOOG fell 0.97% to 314.19, a fifth consecutive lower close, on a day the Nasdaq rose \u2014 now at -0.58R and 7.84 a share above its stop on the day's low. The day -3.09; Q4 +22.97 with +34.64 realised. Heat 1.01R, 1.99R spare. The Fed and Alphabet share Wednesday afternoon.",
  "themes": [
   {
    "kicker": "What's next",
    "right": "the Fed and Alphabet in two days",
    "title": "Wednesday afternoon, and then an inflation print",
    "items": [
     {
      "tone": "c-fomc",
      "tag": "FOMC \u00b7 GOOG",
      "html": "<b>Fed decision and Alphabet Q3 in the same hour</b> \u2014 a hold at 3.75-4.00% is 90.6% priced; Alphabet brings $195-205bn of capex and a $3.00 consensus to a market that has just watched Meta fund its own with $95bn of equity",
      "when": "Wed \u00b7 2026-10-28",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-cb",
      "tag": "PCE \u00b7 AMZN",
      "html": "<b>September PCE and Amazon Q3</b> \u2014 the inflation measure the Fed targets, with crude near a six-month high, and the next hyperscaler to account for its spending",
      "when": "Thu \u00b7 2026-10-29",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-er",
      "tag": "AAPL",
      "html": "<b>iPhone Duo in 28 more countries</b> \u2014 the second wave, with display-crack reports now circulating",
      "when": "Fri \u00b7 2026-10-30",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-cb",
      "tag": "EARNINGS",
      "html": "<b>Apple Q4 and Palantir Q3</b> \u2014 both out of this book now, read for the consumer and the software cycle rather than for exposure",
      "when": "Mon \u00b7 2026-11-02",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-fomc",
      "tag": "VOTE",
      "html": "<b>US midterms</b> \u2014 the date the reported Iran strike plans sit behind, and the deadline the fuel-price politics is working to",
      "when": "Tue \u00b7 2026-11-03",
      "kind": "calendar"
     }
    ],
    "note": "<b>Wednesday is two events in one hour and they pull in opposite directions.</b> A hold at <b>3.75-4.00%</b> is <b>90.6% priced</b>, with <b>about 86% on a December increase</b> and Goldman Sachs expecting one more quarter-point to a <b>4.00-4.25%</b> peak \u2014 so the decision itself is close to known and the statement is the variable. <b>Alphabet reports $195-205bn of capex against a consensus near $3.00 a share</b> in the same window, and it does so having watched Meta answer the funding question with <b>$95bn of equity</b> and get rewarded for it. The test Alphabet faces is therefore sharper than the one Meta faced: not <i>how will you pay</i>, but <i>when does the revenue arrive</i>, which is the question its own chief executive deferred six days ago."
   }
  ],
  "ondeck": "<b>Wednesday is two events in one hour and they pull in opposite directions.</b> A hold at <b>3.75-4.00%</b> is <b>90.6% priced</b>, with <b>about 86% on a December increase</b> and Goldman Sachs expecting one more quarter-point to a <b>4.00-4.25%</b> peak \u2014 so the decision itself is close to known and the statement is the variable. <b>Alphabet reports $195-205bn of capex against a consensus near $3.00 a share</b> in the same window, and it does so having watched Meta answer the funding question with <b>$95bn of equity</b> and get rewarded for it. The test Alphabet faces is therefore sharper than the one Meta faced: not <i>how will you pay</i>, but <i>when does the revenue arrive</i>, which is the question its own chief executive deferred six days ago.",
